{0 World and components}

{!Eon_ecs.World} is the central mutable state every other piece of
[eon_ecs] operates on. It owns three things: entities and their lifecycle,
component registration and per-entity attachment, and a second, entity-less
plane of storage for data and services. This chapter covers all three, using
real component modules from the companion example
({{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/world_example.ml}[world_example.ml]}).

{1 Entities}

An entity is an opaque {!Eon_ecs.Entity_id.t} — a slot index plus a
generation counter, so a stale handle from a destroyed entity can never
silently alias a new one that reused the same slot:

{[
  let world  = Eon_ecs.World.create () in
  let player = Eon_ecs.World.create_entity world in
  Eon_ecs.World.destroy_entity world player
]}

{1 Components: register, then attach}

Components have two distinct lifecycle tiers. {b Registration} is
world-level and happens once, before any entity can carry the component —
{!Eon_ecs.World.register_component} takes a name and a numeric id. {b
Attachment} is per-entity and repeatable — {!Eon_ecs.World.add_component},
[set_component], [get_component], and [remove_component] all key off the
same registered name.

The companion example defines its components as real modules, each
carrying its own type, name, and id, rather than scattering string literals
and bare tuples through the example bodies:

{[
  module Position = struct
    type t = { x : float; y : float }

    let name = "Position"
    let id = 0

    let register world = ignore (Eon_ecs.World.register_component world ~name ~id)
  end

  module Health = struct
    type t = { current : int; max : int }

    let name = "Health"
    let id = 2

    let register world = ignore (Eon_ecs.World.register_component world ~name ~id)
  end
]}

Registering both, once, when a world is created:

{[
  let make_world () =
    let world = Eon_ecs.World.create () in
    Position.register world;
    Health.register world;
    world
]}

{1 The lifecycle: add, get, set, remove}

{[
  open Eon_ecs

  let world  = make_world () in
  let player = World.create_entity world in
  World.add_component world player ~name:Position.name { Position.x = 0.0; y = 0.0 };
  World.add_component world player ~name:Health.name { Health.current = 100; max = 100 };

  (* get_component: Some if the entity currently holds the component. *)
  match World.get_component world player ~name:Health.name with
  | Some (h : Health.t) -> assert (h.current = 100)
  | None -> assert false
]}

[set_component] overwrites the value in place — and it {b promotes to an
add} if the entity didn't already have the component, so it's always safe
to call even on first attachment:

{[
  World.set_component world player ~name:Health.name { Health.current = 80; max = 100 }
]}

{2 Absent vs. never registered — a critical distinction}

A component that's registered but never attached to a given entity is a
completely normal state — [get_component] just returns [None]:

{[
  let enemy = World.create_entity world in
  match World.get_component world enemy ~name:Health.name with
  | None   -> ()          (* expected: enemy never got a Health component *)
  | Some _ -> assert false
]}

A component name that was {b never registered at all} is a different
condition entirely — every one of [add_component]/[set_component]/
[get_component]/[remove_component] raises [Failure] in that case:

{[
  match World.get_component world enemy ~name:"Never_registered" with
  | _ -> assert false
  | exception Failure _ -> ()   (* programmer error: forgot to register *)
]}

{b Never use that exception as a presence check.} It signals a bug (a
missing [register_component] call), not "this entity doesn't have the
component" — that's what the [None] case above is for. Conflating the two
by wrapping calls in a blanket [try ... with _ -> None] hides real
registration bugs behind a result that looks like ordinary absence.

{1 Removal and destruction}

[remove_component] detaches a single component without touching the rest
of the entity:

{[
  World.remove_component world player ~name:Position.name;
  match World.get_component world player ~name:Position.name with
  | None   -> ()
  | Some _ -> assert false
]}

[destroy_entity] detaches {b every} remaining component in one call — no
manual per-component cleanup needed before destroying an entity:

{[
  World.destroy_entity world player;
  World.destroy_entity world enemy
]}

{1 Component generation}

{!Eon_ecs.World.component_generation} returns a monotonically increasing
counter, one per registered component, bumped only when
[add_component]/[remove_component] actually changes that component's
membership on some entity — a value-only [set_component] on an
already-present component leaves it untouched:

{[
  let g0 = World.component_generation world Health.name in

  (* add_component changes membership: generation bumps. *)
  World.add_component world player ~name:Health.name { Health.current = 100; max = 100 };
  let g1 = World.component_generation world Health.name in
  assert (g1 > g0);

  (* set_component on an already-present component changes only the
     value, not membership: generation does NOT bump. *)
  World.set_component world player ~name:Health.name { Health.current = 80; max = 100 };
  let g2 = World.component_generation world Health.name in
  assert (g2 = g1);

  (* remove_component changes membership again: generation bumps. *)
  World.remove_component world player ~name:Health.name;
  let g3 = World.component_generation world Health.name in
  assert (g3 > g2)
]}

Like the rest of the component API, it raises if the name was never
registered — consistent with [get_component]. [eon_ecs] itself has no
internal consumer of this primitive; it exists as a public, additive
building block. [eon_engine]'s [Cached_backend] is the motivating consumer
— it snapshots a query signature's involved components' generations at
fill time and compares them on every read to decide whether a cached
result is still valid, without needing any mutation hook or callback from
[World] itself. Sparse-set length is deliberately not used for this: a
same-tick swap (remove one entity's component, add a different entity's)
leaves length unchanged, which would make a length-keyed cache go stale
silently.

{1 Data and service stores}

Alongside entities and components, {!Eon_ecs.World} carries a second,
entity-less plane of storage — arbitrary values keyed by open polymorphic
variants, for data that doesn't belong to any one entity (a frame counter, a
shared RNG, a physics-world handle):

{[
  let world = World.create () in

  (* Data — read/write, expected to change (e.g. once per frame) *)
  World.add_data world `Frame_count 0;
  World.set_data world `Frame_count 1;
  (match World.get_data world `Frame_count with
   | Some n -> assert (n = 1)
   | None   -> assert false);

  (* Services — registered once, treated as read-mostly thereafter *)
  World.add_service world `Rng (Random.State.make_self_init ());
  match World.get_service world `Rng with
  | Some (_ : Random.State.t) -> ()
  | None -> assert false
]}

[Data] vs. [Service] is a naming convention for intent, not an enforced
distinction — both are backed by the same internal store. This raw plane is
deliberately thin and string-free (open polymorphic variants, not string
keys); [eon_engine] builds a typed, per-resource functor facade on top of
the equivalent plane in its own [World] — see its docs if you're working at
that layer.

